gave  similar results as youth as to what they  had been getting in their plastic base  system and there will be references for  both of these results at the end of the  talk and moving on to the allogeneic  model which is a case study in scaling  out the quantum system from one machine  to 10 machines so this case study was a  collaboration between a thesis and its  European subsidiary Regenesis and the  contact manufacturing organization of  master cell so the object was to  transfer the process of so growing map  sees in the quantum to scale that  process out from one machine to ten  machines to create a larger lot size and  then finally to see if there are any  efficiencies that could be gained and  cutting straight to the chase we found  that there was a 40% cost a 40%  reduction in the cost of goods producing  cells in this way so in the first slide  we show the technology transfer a time  line through to scale out so the first  few weeks were spent simply learning how  to run the quantum and then another six  weeks were used for process optimization  most of this work was on downstream  processing of the self product and  finally the ten quantum runs were done  in the scale out so this is a comparison  of running a single quantum system here  in the Box on the bottom it tells you  how many people are required so you can  see on day one we take two operators to  run the quantum system that's mostly to  prepare the cells and the quantum for  loading with the ten quantum system you  see in the box at the top that two  operators are also required in day zero  and day one or day minus one and day  zero but basically the intermediate  steps can be handled by a single  operator and as I said before the  harvest day is the most difficult and  here we needed more help so finally if  you look at the product that we got you  can see that the flow cytometry profiles  from the comparing the quantum produced  cells to the self stack produce cells  are equal and same the same is true for  the protein secretion so you can see  that we were able to develop a process  that can yield consistent cell qualify  quality and yield and can simplify the  progression from learning to production  and importantly there's a 40% cost  reduction in cost of goods and we were  able to produce large lots of cells so  here are the references for the  lentiviral the retroviral and finally  the scale out of the quantum cell  expansion system thank you very much  Thank You dr. Zeltzer for that  informative presentation it is time for  Q&amp;A if you have a question you'd like to  ask dr. belt sir please do so now just  click on the green Q&amp;A button at the  lower left of the presentation window  type your question into the box it  appears on your screen and click on the  send button I'll answer as many of your  questions as we have time for let's get  started our first question is can you  control the shear stress and the quantum  for the growth of endothelial cells  thanks for that question first of all  there's very little shear stress in the  quantum system that's pure physics that  you've got a quarter inch Inlet line  going into the icy loop and a two inch  in diameter bioreactor there's very  little shear that happens having said  that though we can control the flow rate  of the media through the icy loop with  our pumps and if needed we can even feed  across the membrane without creating  shear stress how can I monitor the  growth of the cells in the quantum so  you can monitor the growth in the in the  quantum by taking a sample from the EEC  sample port so that's a lure lock port  that is sterile so there's a point two  micron filter behind it we recommend  that you monitor the lactate production  and glucose consumption and typically  those measurements are done once every  24 hours has the quant had been used to  culture IPS cells yes the quantum has  been used to successfully expand IPS  cells we haven't done differentiation of  IPS cells yet 